<section class="page-lead page-shell" aria-labelledby="engineering-title">
  <p class="eyebrow">ForgeWire Labs · Research &amp; Engineering</p>
  <h1 id="engineering-title">The hard problems live <em>between components.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead-copy">A model response is one event in a working system. Context may need to last for months. A task may move to another machine. Someone joining a project needs to understand decisions made before they arrived. We study these problems by building, testing, comparing approaches, and recording what the implementation actually supports.</p>
</section>

<section class="engineering-principles page-shell" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
  <div class="section-heading section-heading--stacked">
    <p class="eyebrow">Current investigations</p>
    <h2 id="questions-title">Questions we're working through.</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">Research can lead to a working feature, a simpler design, or a decision not to build something at all.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="engineering-examples">
    <article class="engineering-example engineering-example--wide">
      <p class="example-label">ForgeWire · private development</p>
      <h3>What survives the conversation?</h3>
      <p>Projects, tools, providers, agents, and operational state have different lifetimes. ForgeWire explores how to keep those responsibilities separate while giving people a coherent place to work. Application development remains private; the public overview explains the design and shows selected development interfaces.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/ForgeWire-Overview">Read the public ForgeWire overview <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </article>
    <article class="engineering-example">
      <p class="example-label">Fabric · private development</p>
      <h3>What makes remote execution trustworthy?</h3>
      <p>Remote work needs more than connectivity. Dispatch must be authenticated, authority has to be scoped, and a running process has to be distinguishable from completed work. Fabric tackles those problems as a separate execution control plane. Integration and availability remain private and configuration-dependent.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/ForgeWire-Overview/blob/85d1905b0bd8db420882f00065daff34833dab27/README.md#3-fabric-dispatch-and-remote-execution">Read the public Fabric scope <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </article>
    <article class="engineering-example">
      <p class="example-label">RepoPact Core · released CLI 3.1.3</p>
      <h3>What should remain after a handoff?</h3>
      <p>A change is easier to trust when its reason, authority, acceptance criteria, and verification survive the session that produced it. RepoPact puts those records in version-controlled files and validates their relationships. The released Core CLI and Rust semantic engine make this a working implementation, not only a research question.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/repopact-core">Inspect RepoPact Core <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="evidence-band" aria-labelledby="evidence-heading">
  <div class="page-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Public engineering evidence</p>
    <h2 id="evidence-heading">Engineering examples you can inspect.</h2>
    <div class="evidence-cards">
      <article>
        <p class="example-label">RepoPact Core · released CLI 3.1.3</p>
        <h3>RepoPact Core: one semantic authority.</h3>
        <p>The Rust engine interprets and validates governed repository records. The Python CLI exposes supported commands; Workbench is a separate client of Core, not a competing validator. Different interfaces need to agree about the same work. The stable published package is version-scoped.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/repopact-core/blob/614689b127cf52f801877cc48440f7e45c3709eb/README.md">Read the Core design notes <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="example-label">Anvil · developer preview 0.1.0a0</p>
        <h3>Anvil: planning without pretending to execute.</h3>
        <p>Anvil stores a local planning ledger and typed references to outside work. The developer preview uses a single-writer JSON store. It doesn't claim execution ownership or an audit trail it doesn't provide; its API and schema may change.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/anvil/tree/89ac5df34090e9ffde6a0b790620e9096fb0f15d">Read Anvil's source documentation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="example-label">SkillForge Academy · Windows app 1.3.2</p>
        <h3>SkillForge Academy: learning with a real release boundary.</h3>
        <p>The published Windows application supports certification study and practice. We're developing a broader course-first platform separately. We don't describe beta learning surfaces as features of the v1.3.2 installer.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ForgeWireLabs/skillforge-academy">Inspect the public project <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

